  "articleBody": "Installing decorative crown or wall molding in a bathroom requires extra care compared to a standard bedroom or living room. Everyday steam, shifting temperatures, and out-of-square drywall can quickly cause poor work to pull apart. Here are five frequent mistakes to avoid when planning your bathroom project.\n\n1. Using unsealed raw pine or basic MDF: Standard interior wood and raw MDF soak up steam like sponges, leading to bubbling and blown joints. Always choose moisture-resistant materials like PVC, polyurethane, or thoroughly primed wood sealed on all sides.\n2. Fastening only into drywall: Ceiling corners often have drywall clips or gaps in framing. Finish nails shot only into sheetrock will loosen as moisture fluctuates, causing sagging crown lines.\n3. Ignoring out-of-square corners: Standard 45-degree miters almost never fit bathroom corners cleanly. Inside corners should be coped by hand so the joint remains tight even when walls shift slightly.\n4. Applying standard interior caulk: Cheap painters caulk turns brittle and cracks under steam exposure. Always use flexible, moisture-resistant sealant formulated for bath environments.\n5. Skipping ventilation planning: Venting fans into attic spaces or running weak exhaust units keeps moisture trapped along the ceiling, degrading finishes over time.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nA finish carpenter ensures your molding stays tight for years. If you want seamless crown molding installed in your Denver home, reach out to HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
